<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="types-predict">
  <title>Predict the Output</title>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-predict-fitb">
    <title>Fill-in-the-blank: type the output</title>
    <p>
      The closest match to the CodeRunner prototype. The code is shown
      <em>static</em> (not runnable), and the student types the output into a
      blank. Each blank is checked against an anchored regular expression.
      Extra wrong-answer tests attach feedback to specific misconceptions.
    </p>

    <exercise label="pred-fitb-findmin">
      <title>Trace <c>find_min</c></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Read the program without running it.</p>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        if numbers[i] < best:
            best = numbers[i]
    return best

print(find_min([8, 3, 9, 1, 6]))
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>It prints: <fillin mode="string" characters="6" name="out"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate name="out">
          <test correct="yes">
            <strcmp>1</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Right: <c>best</c> becomes 3, stays 3, becomes 1, stays 1.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <strcmp>6</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>6 is the <em>last</em> element. Does <c>best</c> change on every pass, or only when the <c>if</c> is true?</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <strcmp>8</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>8 is only the starting value. Check what happens when <c>i</c> is 1.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <strcmp>3</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>Good start, but the loop does not stop at the first smaller number.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
            <feedback><p>The function returns one of the list's elements. Which one?</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="pred-fitb-multiline">
      <title>Several lines of output, ending in an error</title>
      <statement>
        <p>What does this program print? Use one blank per line of output.
        If the program stops with an error, write only the name of the error
        (e.g. <c>NameError</c>) in the next blank, and write <c>nothing</c>
        in any blank after that. (A blank cannot be left empty: an empty
        blank always counts as <q>no answer</q>.)</p>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
total = 0
for i in range(1, 4):
    total = total + i
    print(total)
print(min([]))
print("done")
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>Line 1: <fillin mode="string" characters="16"/></p>
        <p>Line 2: <fillin mode="string" characters="16"/></p>
        <p>Line 3: <fillin mode="string" characters="16"/></p>
        <p>Line 4: <fillin mode="string" characters="16"/></p>
        <p>Line 5: <fillin mode="string" characters="16"/></p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate><test correct="yes"><strcmp>1</strcmp></test></evaluate>
        <evaluate><test correct="yes"><strcmp>3</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>2</strcmp><feedback><p>Line 2 prints the running total, not <c>i</c>.</p></feedback></test></evaluate>
        <evaluate><test correct="yes"><strcmp>6</strcmp></test></evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>ValueError(:.*)?</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>(None|0|\[\])</strcmp><feedback><p><c>min</c> of an empty list has no answer, so Python stops with an error.</p></feedback></test>
          <test><strcmp>.*Error.*</strcmp><feedback><p>It is an error, but not that one.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes"><strcmp>(nothing|-)</strcmp></test>
          <test><strcmp>done</strcmp><feedback><p>After an error the program stops. Nothing more is printed.</p></feedback></test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-predict-mcq">
    <title>Multiple choice built from misconceptions</title>
    <p>
      Quicker to answer and to mark, and each distractor can be one named
      misconception with its own feedback. Use it for early checks, and the
      typed version when you want recall rather than recognition.
    </p>
    <exercise label="pred-mcq-tie">
      <title>A tie for the minimum</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python">
          <code><![CDATA[
def find_min_index(numbers):
    best_i = 0
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        if numbers[i] < numbers[best_i]:
            best_i = i
    return best_i

print(find_min_index([4, 2, 7, 2, 9]))
]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>What is printed?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>1</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. The second 2 is not <em>smaller</em> than the first, so <c>best_i</c> stays 1.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>3</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>That would need <c>&lt;=</c>. With strict <c>&lt;</c>, an equal value never replaces the best.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>2</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>2 is the smallest <em>value</em>. The function returns a <em>position</em>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>4</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Index 4 holds the last element. <c>best_i</c> only changes when the <c>if</c> is true.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-predict-codelens">
    <title>CodeLens: predict the trace step by step</title>
    <p>
      CodeLens steps through the program like Python Tutor. In
      <em>checkpoint</em> mode it stops at chosen lines and asks for the value
      of a variable before revealing it. This is the trace-table exercise
      (block 8, exercise 2) made interactive. PreTeXt computes the traces at
      build time (<c>pretext generate trace</c>).
    </p>
    <exercise label="pred-codelens-findmin">
      <title>Trace the running best</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Step through the program. At each checkpoint, type the value of
        <c>best</c> before stepping on.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="codelens" label="codelens-findmin">
        <code><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        if numbers[i] < best:
            best = numbers[i]
    return best

print(find_min([8, 3, 9, 1, 6]))
]]></code>
        <checkpoint line="6" answer-variable="current_frame.best">
          <prompt>The loop is over. What value does <c>best</c> hold now?</prompt>
        </checkpoint>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-predict-run">
    <title>Predict, then run (honour system)</title>
    <p>
      The cheapest form, and the one the PRIMM <q>Run</q> stage expects:
      the student commits to a prediction (written down, or in a separate short-answer exercise), then
      runs the code and compares. Nothing is graded automatically.
    </p>
    <exercise label="pred-then-run">
      <statement>
        <p>Write down what you expect this to print. Then press
        <em>Run</em> and compare. If you were wrong, explain why.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="ac-pred-then-run">
        <code><![CDATA[
numbers = [5, 3, 8, 1]
best = 0
for x in numbers:
    if x < best:
        best = x
print(best)
]]></code>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
